  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nThe pattern shaping up heading into midweek features \nunseasonably strong flow for this close to the summer solstice, \namid a trough in the east and ridge in the west long wave \npattern.\n\nSeveral short wave troughs within the larger long wave trough\nover the east travers mid to late week, the first of which \ndrives a nearly steady-state but unseasonably intense surface \nlow on the order of 990 MB across the midwest on Wednesday, and \nthen the lower Great Lakes Wednesday night into Thursday. A\nmid-level jet max of as high as 90 kts is forecast within a\nnarrow ribbon of tight height and thermal gradients from MT\nsoutheastward through WY, and then eastward across the midwest \nand on through PA, and then up through New England.\n\nThe h85 jet increases to 60 kts Wednesday night amid tight low \nlevel height/pressure gradients, cranking h85 theta E values \ninto the 340s K while associated moisture flux convergence \n(moisture transport up to 500 gm/kgs) contributes greatly to PW\nvalues, which approach two inches Thursday atop surface dew \npoints climbing into the low to mid 70s.\n\nWith the help of veering with height of the strong low-level\nflow, NAM12 Storm Relative Helicities approach 200 m**2/s**2 at\ntimes, with brief spikes into the 400s Wednesday night and /or \nThursday amid low LCLs. While these spikes are not always \nforecast coincident with peak 0-2 and 0-3 km bulk shear in the \n40s kts, a Tornado threat may exist, with the unit-less \nSignificant Tornado Parameter exceeding 2 at times, with \ngenerally a morning and then afternoon peak.\n\nOf particular alarm is the STP spiking to 4 at PKB at 12Z \nThursday off the NAM12, this time with 0-2 and 0-3 km bulk \nshear up around 50 kts and 0-2 and 0-3 km SRH over 500\nm**2/s**2.\n\nThese impressive parameters, with embedded off-the-chart spikes, \nmay be enough to thwart non-diurnal timing or the presence of \nclouds and precipitation through the day inhibiting diurnal \nheating on Thursday.\n\nThe Storm Prediction Center has a Marginal to Slight Risk for\nSevere Thunderstorms into the middle Ohio Valley in their Day 2\noutlook, which depicts the overnight/pre-dawn threat, with all\nthree hazards, especially wind, nudging in from the west. SPC \nhas a Slight Risk for nearly the entire area Thursday, and state\ndamaging wind gusts as the main threat.\n\nWhile the NAM12 may be over-zealous with its Severe and\nTornado parameters, even the GFS peaks STP to 1 at times with\nthe NSHARP algorithm highlighting a Tornado threat off of that\nmodel as well.\n\nThe moisture parameters are certainly high enough for an\nExcessive Rainfall Risk even with fast storm motion, especially\nin poor drainage or highly saturated areas and/or repetitive\nstorms starting early Thursday and potentially building through\nthe day.\n\nThe cold front sags southeastward through the area Thursday\nafternoon and night, but the second short wave trough triggers a\nwave along the front Friday morning. With moisture feeding into\nsouthern portions of the area from a southern stream system,\nalong and south of the confluence zone between the northern and\nsouthern stream systems, the risk of excessive rainfall could \nlinger across southern portions of the area Thursday into \nFriday.\n\nMirroring the Severe Thunderstorm Risks from SPC, the Weather\nPrediction Center edges Marginal to Slight Risks for Excessive\nrainfall into the middle Ohio Valley in their Day 2 outlook, \nwhich depicts the overnight/pre-dawn threat, and then a Slight \nRisk for nearly the entire area Thursday/Thursday night. They \ndepict also depict a Marginal Risk for Excessive rainfall into \nsouthern portions of the area on Friday,\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nHigh pressure sitting over the area this morning, providing the\ncalm before the storms, exits the area today. A short wave and\nweakening surface cold front trough pushes into the middle Ohio\nValley tonight, with the chance for showers and ordinary \nthunderstorms there. With frontolysis early Wednesday morning,\nand then a warm front crossing Wednesday afternoon and evening,\nWednesday turns out mainly dry and warmer.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\n\nThe cold front should push far enough southeast to allow high\npressure to provide dry weather in time for the start of the\nweekend. However, as the weather pattern remains active, another\nshort wave trough and surface cold front brings the next chance\nfor showers and thunderstorms by the time the weekend is out, \nand continuing into the start of the following work week. \n\nThe weather should then at least start to dry out heading into\nthe middle of next week, depending upon the timing of that\nsystem moving through.",
